\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Internal parity grading}
\date{2026-10-20}

\p{Let #{R} be a commutative unital ring and #{A} an associative
unital \vocabk{#{R}-algebra}{ca-0016}. An
\newvocab{internal parity grading} consists of
#{R}-\vocabk{submodules}{ca-0011} #{A_0,A_1\subseteq A}, with
operations inherited from #{A}, such that every #{a\in A} has a unique
decomposition #{a=a_0+a_1}, with #{a_i\in A_i}, and
##{1\in A_0,\qquad A_iA_j\subseteq A_{i+j}.}
Subscripts are added in #{\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z}.
An element of #{A_i} is \newvocab{homogeneous of parity #{i}};
parity #{0} is even and parity #{1} is odd. The zero element lies
in both submodules. Scalars #{r1} belong to #{A_0}.}

\p{The multiplication condition controls the component of a product;
it does not require
#{ab=(-1)^{ij}ba} for #{a\in A_i}, #{b\in A_j}. Nor does it require an
odd element to square to zero. Those are additional equations,
tested explicitly by the [four-cycle](fdip-001M).}

\p{The internal-submodule viewpoint and multiplication rule follow
\citet{Section 1, accepted-manuscript pp. 1-2}{wieser2022graded},
here specialized to the index group #{\mathbb Z/2\mathbb Z}.
Mathlib's \leanref{GradedRing} and \leanref{GradedAlgebra} encode
graded multiplication and compatible scalar data. The chosen
operator decomposition still needs the proof in
\ref{fdip-001K}.}
